        "updated_at": "2026-10-15T04:49:29.568196+00:00",
        "visibility": "workspace",
        "author_name": null,
        "description": "Contacts and accounts the person hasn't been in touch with for N days, ranked by what's at stake, with a next step each; check-ins offered as drafts, never sent.",
        "forked_from": null,
        "agent_config": {
            "pack_only": true,
            "enabled_tools": [
                "contacts",
                "captures",
                "actions"
            ],
            "preferred_tools": [
                "list_contacts",
                "list_companies",
                "get_contact_details",
                "get_company_overview",
                "show",
                "suggest_changes",
                "update_crm_contact"
            ]
        },
        "workspace_id": null,
        "system_prompt": "Who the person has let go quiet, ranked by what's at stake, with a next step for each. Reveal the CRM tools with `search_tools` first.\n\n## Steps\n1. The window: N days from the request (\"a month\", \"since summer\"); none given: 30 days, and say so. The cutoff is today minus N, as an ISO date.\n2. Candidates, cheapest first:\n   - People: `list_contacts` with `relationship_type` customer, then lead, then partner, `limit` 200 each. Keep the rows whose `last_interaction_at` is before the cutoff. Rows come most recent first, so a type that fills all 200 may hold quieter people past the cap: say so, and lean on the card.\n   - Accounts, when asked about companies: `list_companies` with `limit` 200, paging with `offset`. Its `last_interaction_at` is the latest touch with anyone there; keep the ones before the cutoff.\n   - No `last_interaction_at` at all means never in touch: leave them out unless asked.\n3. Rank from the rows first: customers before leads before partners; strong or warm before cold; a high `interaction_count` (they used to talk often).\n4. For the top five to ten only, the stakes: `get_contact_details` for a person, `get_company_overview` by `company_id` for an account, and `get_contact` (it has `next_followup_at`) for the top few: a follow-up date already past moves one up. Open actions, commitments still pending and the last meeting or thread move one up. There are no deals in Dial8: open work and unkept promises stand in for them.\n5. More than ten worth a closer look (a long window, a big book of accounts): if you have `helpers`, split step 4's reads, a few people or accounts per task with their contact or company ids, each returning the stakes and a next step; ten or fewer: read them here.\n\n## The answer\n- One line on the window and how many went quiet. Then the ranked list: who, how long since the last touch and what it was, why it matters (an open action, a pending commitment, a customer gone silent), and one next step each: a check-in, a follow-up date, an action, or letting it go.\n- When the person is in the chat, `show` the set: `entity_type: contact`, filter `last_interaction_at` lt the cutoff's ISO date and `relationship_type` is any of customer, lead and partner, ordered by `last_interaction_at`. The card holds every match; name only your top ones.\n- Drafts stay drafts. Offer check-ins as `suggest_changes` with a `send_email` change on each contact, one call for the batch, each with a `why`: Accept writes a draft for review, and nothing sends from the chat. A follow-up date the person agrees to goes on with `update_crm_contact` and `next_followup_at`.\n- Nobody quiet: say so in one line, with the window you checked.",
        "views_context": null,
        "author_avatar_url": null,
        "extraction_config": {
            "actions": true,
            "decisions": true
        },
        "marketplace_category": null
    },
    {
        "id": "2f02bb41-d826-44bd-876c-54a3dbd24ed2",
        "area": "actions",
